#---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------#
Context means controlling the k8s cluster from my local machine
Context is a set of access parameters that contains information about the cluster, user, and namespace. 
It allows you to switch between different clusters and namespaces easily.
Context is defined in the kubeconfig file, which is usually located at ~/.kube/config.
Context is a combination of cluster and account information that allows you to access a specific Kubernetes cluster.
#---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------#
Setting up a cluster
#-------------------#
In ubuntu run "kubectl cluster-info" which gives IP of the machine

In local machine, run the following command to set up a cluster:
kubectl config set-cluster <cluster-name> --server=<api-server-url(publicIP:portnum)> --insecure-skip-tls-verify=true

Example:
kubectl config set-cluster dec-2025 --server=https://13.234.35.226:6443 --insecure-skip-tls-verify=true

Note: If we check in config file, the cluster will be set [C:\Users\dell\.kube\config]

In local machine, run the following command to set up an account:
kubectl config set-credentials <credential-name> --token=<paste-token>

Note: To find token in ubuntu machine run "kubectl describe secret <service-account-name>{service-account-readonly-token}"
Note: Replace service-account-name with service-account-readonly-token while applying the command 

